---
// The guest privacy notice. Both sites render this one file: the guest site as
// its `/privacy` page (`cire/invites/src/pages/privacy.astro`), and
// cireweddings.com as `/privacy/guests`
// (`cire/landing/src/pages/privacy/guests.astro`). Edit the notice here, and
// move `updated` with any change to what it says.
//
// Served to the guests of EVERY wedding, so it names no couple.
//
// The split it describes is the one the code implements. Hosts choose what to
// ask and what to do with the answers. We hold the data for them — but we also
// decide, alone and for everyone, how long guest data survives the wedding
// (`cire/api/src/services/retention.ts`), how the site is secured, and what
// telemetry it emits. Those are controller decisions, so the notice says so.
import { LEGAL_ENTITY, isPlaceholder } from '@shared/legal'
import { CATEGORY_META } from './consent-categories'
import { CONSENT_VENDORS, vendorsInCategory } from './consent-vendors'

interface Props {
  /**
   * Heading level of the notice's sections: 2 where the notice is the page,
   * 3 where it sits under a heading of its own. Sub-sections take the next
   * level down.
   */
  level?: 2 | 3
}

const { level = 2 } = Astro.props
const Section = level === 2 ? 'h2' : 'h3'
const Sub = level === 2 ? 'h3' : 'h4'

const updated = '2026-10-21'

// The third-party lists are generated from the consent vendor registry, so the
// notice cannot disagree with what the guest site's consent framework governs:
// what the site stores or loads to work at all, and the third parties a guest
// switches on.
const necessaryVendors = vendorsInCategory('necessary')
const switchedVendors = CONSENT_VENDORS.filter((vendor) => vendor.enforcement === 'gated')

// The `choices` slot takes the guest site's button that opens its privacy
// choices. Those choices are stored on the guest site's own origin, so
// cireweddings.com passes nothing and the notice points at the site's footer.
const hasChoices = Astro.slots.has('choices')

// Highlight a detail only while THAT detail is still a placeholder. A page-wide
// flag would mark a filled field as pending the moment any other one wasn't.
const ph = (value: string) => (isPlaceholder(value) ? 'placeholder' : undefined)
---
<div data-guest-notice>
  <p class="meta">Last updated: {updated}</p>

  <p>
    Each wedding invitation sent with Cire is a private website (“the site” in this
    notice). It asks for the small amount of information your hosts need to plan the
    day and cater for you. This notice explains what is collected, why, how long it
    is kept, who decides what, and the rights you have over it.
  </p>

  <Section>Who decides what</Section>
  <p>
    Two parties are involved, and it matters which one you need.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Your hosts</strong> — the couple, and whoever is helping them organise.
      They chose to invite you, decide what to ask you, see your answers, and use them
      to run their wedding. Almost anything you might want changed — your name, your
      household, whether you are on the list at all — is theirs to change, and they are
      the fastest route to it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Us</strong>, <span class={ph(LEGAL_ENTITY.name)}>{LEGAL_ENTITY.name}</span>, who build and
      run the site. We hold and process guest information on your hosts’ instructions.
      We also make some decisions on our own account and for every wedding alike — how
      long guest data survives the event, how the site is secured, and what technical
      telemetry it produces — and for those decisions the responsibility is ours, not
      your hosts’.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    You can contact us about anything in this notice at
    <a href={`mailto:${LEGAL_ENTITY.contactEmail}`}>
      <span class={ph(LEGAL_ENTITY.contactEmail)}>{LEGAL_ENTITY.contactEmail}</span></a>, whether or not you have
    spoken to your hosts first.
  </p>

  <Section>What is collected</Section>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Guest names</strong> — the names on your household invitation, and the
      name of anyone a guest is allowed to bring and chooses to.
    </li>
    <li><strong>RSVP status</strong> — whether each named guest is attending each event.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Dietary requirements and access needs</strong> — anything you choose
      to type into the free-text field (for example allergies, intolerances, or
      requirements that may reveal health information, or religious or
      belief-based dietary needs).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>When your household first opened the invitation</strong> — a single
      timestamp, shown to your hosts as a “Sent / Opened” marker so they know whether an
      invitation arrived. Not a record of every visit, and not what you looked at.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>When your household’s replies changed</strong> — each time a reply is
      sent or changed, which guest and event it was for and when, but not the answer
      itself. Your hosts see it as a “new” mark beside that reply, and the couple and
      anyone helping them plan get a daily count of changes by email, with no names in it.
      When one of your hosts sets a reply’s status for you, the record also notes which
      host did it, so the couple can tell who changed what; that change is not shown as new.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Which of you sent each reply</strong> — each reply and change records the
      guest who sent it. When your invitation names more than one guest, it asks which of
      you is answering. Your household sees “Answered by” and that guest’s first name under
      each reply, and your hosts see it too. It rests on your word, not on a check of who
      you are.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Where you sit, if your hosts plan seating</strong> — the table each guest
      is at for each event, set by your hosts. They see it, and may print it or send it
      to the venue; other guests never do.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your email address, if you or your hosts add one</strong> — so your hosts can
      send you news about the wedding through us. You can add, change or remove your own on
      your invitation. If your hosts add it, they confirm to us that you agreed, and your
      invitation shows it as added by them. With it we keep who added it, when, and the wording
      that was agreed to. We use it for nothing else.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Gifts, if you give one through the registry</strong> — what your household
      reserves or marks as bought from your hosts’ list, and any money you send: the amount,
      the currency, the day, and the name and note you choose to add. Your hosts see these
      so they can thank you; other guests never do.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Technical records</strong> — the session cookie that keeps you signed in
      to your invitation after you enter your code, and server logs kept to keep the
      site secure and working.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Nothing else is asked for or knowingly collected through the site. You only ever
    fill in details for the members of your own household, and for a guest your hosts
    have let you bring.
  </p>

  <Sub>A note on dietary information</Sub>
  <p>
    Some dietary or access details are treated as a protected category of information
    — under the <em>Privacy Act 1988</em> (Cth) as “sensitive information”, and under
    the GDPR and UK GDPR as “special category” data — because they can reveal your
    health or your religious beliefs. Both regimes say such information should be
    collected only with your explicit consent (APP 3.3; GDPR Art. 9(2)(a)). The field
    is therefore optional, you tick a box to confirm before it is submitted, and a record of
    which version of this wording you agreed to is stored alongside it. If you give your
    answer to your hosts another way — over the phone, say — they can record it for you,
    and what is stored then is their confirmation that you agreed, not yours. If you
    are bringing a guest, you can give their dietary requirements only by ticking a
    separate box confirming that they agreed, and what is stored for them is your
    confirmation and the version of the wording you confirmed. Either way the person
    the information is about can withdraw it with us directly. Please don’t include any
    health or other sensitive detail you’d rather not share — a simple note such as
    “vegetarian” or “no nuts” is all that is needed.
  </p>

  <Section>Why it is used, and on what basis</Section>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Guest names &amp; RSVP status</strong> — to manage invitations, confirm
      numbers, and organise seating and logistics. Your hosts have a legitimate
      interest in running the event they invited you to, and providing the invitation
      is what our contract with them requires of us.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Dietary requirements / access needs</strong> — to cater safely and
      accommodate your needs on the day. Because this can include protected
      information, the basis is your <strong>explicit consent</strong>, given when you
      tick the box and submit your RSVP, and withdrawable at any time. For a guest
      someone brings, it is that guest’s explicit consent, as confirmed by the person
      bringing them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your email address</strong> — so your hosts can send you news about the
      wedding. The basis is your consent: given when you add it on your invitation, or given
      to your hosts when they add it. Remove the address on your invitation to withdraw it at
      any time; after that we will not email it about this wedding, even if it is added again,
      unless you add it back yourself.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The first-opened timestamp</strong> — so your hosts can tell a delivered
      invitation from one that never arrived. Their legitimate interest in administering
      their own wedding; you can ask for it to be removed.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Technical records</strong> — to keep your session working and the site
      secure. Our legitimate interest in a site that functions and is not abused.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Your information is never sold, and never used for marketing or advertising.
  </p>

  <Section>If a guest is bringing you</Section>
  <p>
    Your hosts may let a guest bring someone with them. If that is you, the guest who is
    bringing you gave us your name, and may give us your RSVP and — only if you agreed —
    your dietary requirements. One of your hosts may also record your RSVP and dietary
    requirements on your behalf; when they record your dietary requirements, what is
    stored is their confirmation that you agreed. You have no invitation code of your own
    and may never see the invitation itself, which is why we ask that guest to pass this
    notice on to you.
  </p>
  <p>
    We hold your details for your hosts on the same terms as every other guest’s, set out
    in this notice, and delete them on the same schedule. Your name and RSVP rest on your
    hosts’ legitimate interest in running their wedding. Your dietary requirements rest on
    your explicit consent, and what is stored is the confirmation of the guest who is
    bringing you that you agreed, with the version of the wording they confirmed.
  </p>
  <p>
    You have the same rights as any other guest, set out below, and do not need a code to
    use them: ask your hosts, or email us at
    <a href={`mailto:${LEGAL_ENTITY.contactEmail}`}>
      <span class={ph(LEGAL_ENTITY.contactEmail)}>{LEGAL_ENTITY.contactEmail}</span></a>, to
    correct your name, withdraw your agreement to your dietary requirements being kept, or
    have your details deleted. The guest who is bringing you can also change your name or
    remove you until RSVPs close.
  </p>

  <Section>Who can see it</Section>
  <p>
    Your hosts, and the people they have asked to help — a caterer or a venue, for
    instance — strictly so those people can do their job. Your hosts choose who that
    is; we don’t.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your email address, if one is held, is seen by your hosts and everyone helping them with
    the guest list. Your invitation shows it to whoever opens it with your household’s code and
    says they are you, so that you can check and correct it, and never shows one member’s
    address to someone who says they are another.
  </p>
  <p>
    On our side, the site and its data are hosted on <strong>Cloudflare</strong>, which
    provides hosting, the database and security. Beyond that, information is disclosed
    only where the law requires it.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you send money through the registry, the payment is taken by <strong>Stripe</strong>
    on your hosts’ own Stripe account. Stripe collects your card details and the name and
    email you give it, under its own privacy policy; we never see your card, and we tell
    Stripe nothing about you beyond the amount.
  </p>

  <Sub>Where your information is stored</Sub>
  <p>
    Our primary region is <strong>Australia</strong> — the site is operated from there,
    and the infrastructure supporting its security is pinned to Sydney. Some technical records — server logs, error and
    performance telemetry containing no guest content — are processed by providers
    outside Australia in the course of running the service.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you are in the EU or the UK, this means your information is transferred outside
    that area to Australia. You can ask us what protections are in place for that
    transfer, and we will tell you.
  </p>

  <Section>Cookies and content from other companies</Section>
  <p>
    There is no personal tracking on the site: no advertising or tracking cookies, and no
    analytics. The only storage the invite uses is <strong>strictly necessary</strong>,
    and it needs no choice from you:
  </p>
  <ul>
    {necessaryVendors.map((vendor) => (
      <li>
        <strong>{vendor.name}</strong> — {vendor.purpose}
        {vendor.transfer ? <> Data goes to {vendor.transfer}.</> : null}
        {vendor.privacyUrl ? (
          <> <a href={vendor.privacyUrl} rel="noreferrer">Their privacy policy</a>.</>
        ) : null}
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <Sub>The other companies involved</Sub>
  <p>
    Two parts of the invite are served by other companies, and each has its own
    switch. When one of them loads, that company receives your IP address and
    browser details, because that is inherent in your browser fetching something
    from their servers. So <strong>each stays off until you allow it</strong>, and you
    can allow one without the other.
  </p>
  <ul>
    {switchedVendors.map((vendor) => (
      <li>
        <strong>{CATEGORY_META[vendor.category].title}</strong> — {vendor.purpose}
        {vendor.transfer ? <> Data goes to {vendor.transfer}.</> : null}
        <> <em>Off until you allow it; stops loading again if you switch it off.</em></>
        {vendor.privacyUrl ? (
          <> <a href={vendor.privacyUrl} rel="noreferrer">Their privacy policy</a>.</>
        ) : null}
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <Sub>Changing your mind</Sub>
  <div data-notice-choices>
    {hasChoices ? (
      <>
        <p>
          You can review or change these choices at any time — there is a
          <strong>Privacy Choices</strong> link in the footer of every page, and here:
        </p>
        <p>
          <slot name="choices" />
        </p>
      </>
    ) : (
      <p>
        You can review or change these choices at any time — there is a
        <strong>Privacy Choices</strong> link in the footer of every page of the site.
      </p>
    )}
  </div>
  <p>
    Turning something off stops it loading from then on. Anything already sent while it
    was switched on has already left your browser and cannot be recalled. The venue map
    and the moodboard stay off until you allow them, and load only when you open an
    event’s details after that — so unless you have done both, nothing has gone to that
    company.
  </p>

  <Section>How long it is kept</Section>
  <p>
    Guest information — names, RSVPs, dietary needs, email addresses — is deleted
    <strong>1 year after the final wedding event</strong>, after which it is
    permanently removed from our systems. Now and then it goes up to 30 days later,
    while we finish the record of your hosts’ gifts. That period is set by us and applies to every
    wedding; your hosts cannot extend it. You can ask for your own information to be
    deleted sooner.
  </p>
  <p>
    When that happens your hosts keep a record of their gifts with no names in it: how
    many were reserved and bought from their list, and the amount, currency and day of
    each money gift. It stays with their wedding until they delete it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The record of when your household’s replies changed is kept for
    <strong>90 days</strong>, or less if the rest of your information goes first.
  </p>
  <p>
    When you remove your email address it comes off your guest record at once, though copies
    can remain in your hosts’ saved guest-list history until that is deleted on the schedule
    above. We keep a fingerprint of the removed address, not the address itself, so that we can
    recognise it and not email it again, and delete that with the rest of the wedding’s guest
    information.
  </p>

  <Section>Your rights</Section>
  <p>You can ask to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Access</strong> the information held about you;</li>
    <li><strong>Correct</strong> anything wrong or out of date;</li>
    <li><strong>Delete</strong> your information;</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Withdraw your consent</strong> to the dietary and access information, or to your
      email address being used, at any time — this doesn’t undo anything done while it stood.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you are in the EU or the UK, the GDPR and UK GDPR give you these rights plus the
    right to object to processing based on legitimate interests, the right to a copy of
    your information in a portable form, and the right to restrict processing while a
    dispute is resolved. We honour all of them wherever you are; they are set out
    separately only because the law that names them is.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ask your hosts, who can act on most of it immediately, or email us at
    <a href={`mailto:${LEGAL_ENTITY.contactEmail}`}>
      <span class={ph(LEGAL_ENTITY.contactEmail)}>{LEGAL_ENTITY.contactEmail}</span></a> and we will act on it
    ourselves and tell your hosts only what we must. We respond as soon as we
    reasonably can, and within a month at the outside.
  </p>

  <Section>Complaints</Section>
  <p>
    We would rather resolve a concern directly. If you are in Australia and are not
    satisfied, you can contact the
    <span class={ph(LEGAL_ENTITY.regulator)}>{LEGAL_ENTITY.regulator}</span> at
    <a href="https://www.oaic.gov.au/" rel="noreferrer">oaic.gov.au</a>. If you are in
    the EU or the UK, you can complain to your local data protection authority instead.
  </p>

  <Section>Changes to this notice</Section>
  <p>
    If how your information is handled changes, this page is updated and the
    “last updated” date above revised.
  </p>
</div>
